[10/15/2026-07:11:25] [TRT] [E] IBuilderConfig::setFlag: Error Code 3: API Usage Error (Parameter check failed, condition: builderFlag != BuilderFlag::kEXCLUDE_LEAN_RUNTIME || flags[BuilderFlag::kVERSION_COMPATIBLE]. kEXCLUDE_LEAN_RUNTIME can only be set if kVERSION_COMPATIBLE is set. In apiCheckAndSetFlag at /_src/optimizer/api/builderConfig.cpp:185)
    EXCLUDE_LEAN_RUNTIME alone                           56,044 B

    the embedded lean runtime costs 100 MiB on a 55 KiB engine
    -> `EXCLUDE_LEAN_RUNTIME` takes it back to within 4,096 B of the baseline, against a
       100 MiB surcharge: you keep version compatibility and ship the runtime
       separately (04-Feature/LeanAndDispatchRuntime).
       Not *byte for byte*: rebuilding the same configuration already moves the size by ~136 B,
       because the builder times tactics and a tie can break either way.
    -> and alone it is a **silent no-op**. No error, no warning, same plan. It only ever
       subtracts something `VERSION_COMPATIBLE` added.
============================== End   [case_version_compatible_and_lean_runtime]
============================== Start [case_strip_plan,(),{}]
    small network (32 conv filters)                      56,044 ->       79,820 B  (BIGGER)
    weight-heavy network (+2.4 M matmul weights)      9,508,276 ->      110,340 B  (86x smaller)
    -> the flag is worth it exactly when weights dominate the plan, and counterproductive when
       they do not. Check the ratio before adopting it; and remember the weights have to come
       back via a refit at load time (04-Feature/WeightStripping).
============================== End   [case_strip_plan]
============================== Start [case_cache_flags,(),{}]
    flags                                 build   timing cache after the build
    (none)                                3.94s                     26,787 B
    DISABLE_TIMING_CACHE                  4.01s                        198 B
    DISABLE_COMPILATION_CACHE             4.07s                        318 B

    -> **both** flags leave the cache essentially empty (135x and 84x smaller).
       That is the surprise, and it says what a `timing` cache actually holds. Reading the three
       sizes as header + timings + compiled kernels:
           header alone                  ~    198 B  (timing cache off)
           + tactic timings              ~    120 B
           + JIT-compiled kernels        ~ 26,469 B  (98.8% of the file)
       So the thing everyone calls `the timing cache` is overwhelmingly a *compilation* cache;
       the tactic timings it is named after are a rounding error. That is also why
       `DISABLE_COMPILATION_CACHE` shrinks a file that has `timing` in its name.
    Turn either off to make a build reproducible (07-Tool/Polygraphy/More/12-TacticsAndReproducibility)
    or to prove a stale cache is the culprit. Build time is the cost, and on a real model it is minutes.
============================== End   [case_cache_flags]
